til only a silver edge is left of its brightness, it becomes a towering mass of black thunder-cloud. [Originally New Eng.]

{{smaller block|On either hand a sullen rear of woes,<br>Whose garnered lightnings none could guess,<br>Piling its ''thunder-heads'', and muttering "Cease!"
{{right|''Lowell'', Under the Old Elm, vii. 2.}}}}

'''thunder-headed''' (thun′dėr-hed″ed), ''a.'' Pertaining to a thunder-head; like a thunder-head: as, ''thunder-headed'' clouds.

'''thunder-house''' (thun′dėr-hous), ''n.'' A small model of a house with electric conductors so arranged as to show, when a discharge is passed through them, how a building may be injured by lightning.

'''thundering''' (thun′dėr-ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''thunder'', ''v.''] The report of a discharge of lightning; thunder.

{{smaller block|Intreat the Lord . . . that there be no more mighty ''thunderings'' and hail.
{{right|Ex. ix. 28.}}}}

'''thundering''' (thun′dėr-ing), ''p. a.'' 1. Producing or characterized by a loud rumbling or rattling noise, as that of thunder or artillery; loud.—2. Unusual; extraordinary; great; tremendous: used as an intensive. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|He goes a ''thundering'' pace, that you would not think it possible to overtake him.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 420.}}}}

{{smaller block|I was drawing a ''thundering'' fish out of the water, so very large that it made my rod crack again.
{{right|''Tom Brown'', Works, I. 219.}}}}

{{smaller block|Haint they cut a ''thunderin''' swarth?
{{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 1st ser., i.}}}}

